      question: "How do Shakespeare and Browning present power and control differently in 'Macbeth' and 'My Last Duchess'?",
      answer: "Both texts feature men who destroy women to maintain control. In 'Macbeth', Lady Macbeth initially holds psychological power over Macbeth — 'Was the hope drunk wherein you dressed yourself?' — but Duncan's murder ultimately corrupts and destroys her. In 'My Last Duchess', the Duke describes having his wife murdered ('I gave commands; / Then all smiles stopped together') in casual conversation, demonstrating absolute, aristocratic power over life and death. Browning's dramatic monologue gives the Duke full voice but implicates him through what he reveals inadvertently. Shakespeare's dramatic form shows the consequences of power — the sleepwalking scene, Macbeth's increasingly desperate tyranny. Browning's poem is chilling because the Duke faces no consequence — he has already chosen his next wife. Both writers examine how power corrupts relationships, but Shakespeare shows collapse while Browning shows cold, unshaken control.",
