      answer: "Dickens wrote 'A Christmas Carol' in 1843, at a time when industrialisation had created enormous wealth alongside terrible poverty. The New Poor Law (1834) had introduced workhouses, which Dickens loathed — Scrooge's line 'Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?' satirises the attitudes of the wealthy who preferred institutions to charity. Child labour was common; Tiny Tim's disability may reflect poverty-related illness. Dickens's novella was deliberately priced low (five shillings) to reach the widest possible audience — a political as well as literary choice. The novella was part of a broader campaign: Dickens visited the Field Lane Ragged School (for homeless children) the same year.",
