        "Priestley systematically exposes the Birling family's self-interest. Each interrogation reveals a different facet of class privilege. The Inspector's final speech — 'fire and blood and anguish' — warns that ignoring social responsibility leads to collective catastrophe. Writing in 1945, Priestley was arguing for the welfare state and collective responsibility that post-war Britain was about to build.",
