      answer: "Jekyll's experiments represent Victorian science's expanding ambition to understand and reshape human nature — a project that directly challenged religious accounts of the soul as God-given and fixed. Jekyll's goal — to 'separate' the good and evil elements of himself — reflects Enlightenment-era confidence that science can improve the human condition. However, Stevenson inverts this: the experiment produces not improvement but regression and destruction. Hyde is the consequence of science without moral constraint — what happens when the rational removes its ethical limits. Utterson's rational, legal world tries and fails to explain the supernatural events around Jekyll. Lanyon's reaction — he dies of shock after witnessing Hyde transform into Jekyll — suggests that science confronted with the transgression of natural order cannot survive the encounter. Stevenson suggests that some boundaries (between the human and the animalistic, between self and other) should not be crossed — a conservative response to Victorian scientific confidence.",
