        "The conflict between science and religion in the novella is embedded in its premise: can science separate the moral components of a person? Jekyll believes so; Stevenson shows it cannot. Hyde grows stronger — repression and scientific division produce a more dangerous darkness. The novel is partly a warning against Victorian scientific positivism: not all knowledge is safe to pursue.",
