      question: "How do poets in the Power and Conflict cluster present the relationship between the individual and power? Compare at least two poems. (8 marks)",
      answer: "Power and Conflict poetry consistently explores how the individual relates to larger forces of political, military or natural power. In 'Ozymandias', the tyrant's power is presented as an illusion: 'Look on my works, ye Mighty, and despair!' — surrounded by empty desert. The individual (Ozymandias) believed himself supreme; time and nature expose this as vanity. Shelley's political context (Napoleon's tyranny) is clear. In 'Checking Out Me History', Agard's individual 'I' actively resists the power of colonial education: 'I carving out me identity.' The individual reclaims power through knowledge and language — the Caribbean dialect itself is an assertion against colonial power. Together these poems show power operating in two directions: as oppression of the individual (colonial education, military authority) and as individual resistance or refusal. In both cases, the individual's relationship with power is a central act of identity formation.",
