      answer: "Mrs Birling is Priestley's portrait of upper-class moral blindness. As chair of the Brumley Women's Charity Organisation, she refused Eva Smith's request for help — judging her 'claim' as 'not a deserving case.' This is a devastating irony: a charity that is more concerned with worthiness than need. Mrs Birling shows no remorse, hiding behind class prejudice ('She was claiming elaborate fine feelings and claims that were simply absurd in a girl of that class'). Her insistence that the father of Eva's child should bear full responsibility rebounds catastrophically when she realises it is Eric. Her attitude to responsibility — 'naturally I refused' — is the direct opposite of the Inspector's message. Priestley presents her as utterly unreachable by moral argument, representing a class too invested in its own superiority to accept shared responsibility. Even after the Inspector's departure, she dismisses everything: her position is calcified.",
